{
  "captured_at": "2026-11-12T02:16:54.176037+00:00",
  "purpose": "Local storage prototype; no production schema changes",
  "target": "Integer sample references, parent placements, shared relative strings stored once in a dictionary heap with digest lookup keys, filename overrides only when needed",
  "methodology": [
    "Six package cohorts contain all existing direct-child locations for the selected catalog identities. Versions lacking locations contribute no member rows.",
    "Lodash and Next.js were exported in sequential five-identity batches, not one database snapshot. Other cohorts use one COPY statement. Concurrent production changes are possible.",
    "ESLint and other timed-out exports were excluded; successful retries replaced partial files.",
    "Broad sample uses TABLESAMPLE SYSTEM(0.001), seed 905. Page clustering and missing reuse make its distinct-path fraction unsuitable for population extrapolation.",
    "All five layouts retain original metadata, timestamps, relationship types, and embedded locator strings. Every original column reconstructed exactly.",
    "Dictionary layouts include the full parent-placement and relative-path table/index costs. Indexes are freshly built; production bloat is not modeled.",
    "Identity-only text and binary tables are measured separately. They are not full samples tables and their bytes are excluded from effective location-layout totals.",
    "Parent placements and sample IDs are synthetic local mappings. A production migration must resolve real IDs, preserve location uniqueness and extraction semantics, and handle unresolved parents.",
    "A dictionary stores each relative string once in its heap; its unique text index also contains the string. This is counted in the measurements. No directory-component interning was attempted.",
    "Space scenarios multiply each cohort bytes/location by the primary estimated row count. They are sensitivity scenarios, not estimates of global path cardinality.",
    "Space scenarios assume no ZFS compression savings and exclude WAL, change capture, sort space, growth, snapshot retention, and reserve. Ingestion throughput was not benchmarked.",
    "A second measured dictionary uses a unique 32-byte path digest index, avoiding the full-string index copy. Exact-string verification and explicit collision rejection are requirements for a production writer; no writer is implemented here."
  ],
